  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 301 AM EDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nPlenty of uncertainty in the forecast today, yet, depending on which \nsolution verifies, heavy rainfall may prove the most likely hazard\nthis evening along the Indiana-Michigan state line. This is in \naddition to the damaging wind gust and tornado risk from severe \nthunderstorms this afternoon (low confidence) and overnight (medium \nconfidence).\n\nAs it goes here in northern Indiana, rounds of storms are highly \ndependent on how any convection earlier in the day evolves. Early \nthis morning, the area to watch is over the ND-SD state line where a \ngrowing complex of storms exists. This complex drifts east and into \nnortheast IL and into IN this afternoon. Preexisting high pressure \nmay be the element guidance is struggling with, especially in \ncontrast to the incoming warm front which is timed well to support \nafternoon convection. Mid-level lapse rates of about 6.8 C/km could \nbe better. Aloft, a 45-knot jet could be enough to sustain storms, \nyet the best upper-level support is well north across the upper \nGreat Lakes. Ensemble high resolution guidance generally suggests a \nweakening line of convection arrives by mid-afternoon and presents a \nnon-zero chance for damaging wind gusts amid 1500 j/kg of CAPE and \nabout 30 knots of shear. \n\nGiven the anticipated modest state of this afternoon's convection, \nround two overnight could prove more potent. Starting with \nthe heavy rain concern, multiple forecast models are latching onto a \ncomplex of rain and storms that becomes anchored over south-\ncentral Lake Michigan extending east through the Indiana-Michigan \nstate line. Storms develop along the warm front amid a strengthening \nlow-level jet. Michiana communities from La Porte and Michigan city, \neast to the greater Elkhart-Goshen metro areas could be at risk for \nurban flooding from rain rates of at least 1\" per hour \n(likely more) and rainfall totals in excess of 2-2.5 \ninches if forecasts verify. Boundary-parallel flow (which is \nespecially weak and convergent at the surface), 500-mb dew point \ndepressions of 1 to 2 C indicate a very deep moisture profile, \nalong with warm cloud depths nearing 14,000 ft. Swift 850-mb flow of \nabout 30-40 knots and generally dry recent conditions may work to \nreduce the flood risk. Nonetheless, if training storms can \ndevelop, heavy rain and localized urban flooding are a concern \ntonight.\n\nIn addition to the flooding threat, any supercells that develop in \nthe vicinity of the warm front pose a risk for all hazards including \ntornadoes. Lapse Rates once again pose a challenge, as does overall \nstorm evolution, but 0-1km SRH of nearly 300 m2/s2 results in \nshallow but looping hodographs. The previously mentioned \nmoisture-rich atmosphere may prove challenging for hail, but as \nstorms congeal into a line, damaging wind gusts would become the \nprimary hazard through early Monday morning. \n\nWe'll face another possible round of severe thunderstorms late \nMonday afternoon and evening as energetic upper-level flow remains in \nplace. This too could pose a heavy rain risk with many of the same \ningredients in place. Tuesday will be worth watching for more \nstorms until high pressure finally noes in by Wednesday to steer \nthis active pattern elsewhere. Late-week low pressure brings another \nchance for storms. High temperatures will be near to slightly below \nnormal through the 7-day period.",
